{
  "abstract": "For a directed strongly regular graph with $\\lambda=1$, fix a root, its out-neighborhood $O$, and the exterior $F$. We identify the left kernel of the incidence matrix $X=A[O,F]$ with a zero-sum invariant subspace of the adjacency matrix supported in the root and $O$. The predecessor-function spectrum then proves that $X$ has full row rank unless $t\\in\\{2,\\mu,2\\mu\\}$. At $t=2\\mu$ with $\\mu\\ge2$, its kernel consists of left $+1$ eigenvectors supported in $O$. For the unresolved parameters $(27,7,4,1,2)$, a triangle-incidence inequality excludes this kernel. We further prove that every incoming multigraph on the seven out-neighbors is connected and has at least eight distinct edges. Connectedness uses a complete census of 690,120 labelled frames, independent column-domain enumeration, and 74 independently checked DRAT refutations; the support bound uses two exhaustive incidence screens. For $\\lambda=1,\\mu=2,t>2$, we also prove that every exterior is strongly connected, and primitive when $k\\ge5$. Finally, an odd-unicyclic incidence pivot eliminates seven physical exterior rows at order 27 with integral coefficients. An exact commutator identity shows that only the twelve remaining exterior rows require two-walk equations. Singular incidence is retained in the general reconstruction. Standard-library checks cover 2,096 rooted orientations of known graphs, and independent complete-domain and reconstruction audits accompany the finite proofs. The result supplies necessary structure and an exact completion interface; neither target existence nor nonexistence is established.",
